drm/edid: keep the blocks read when a later extension cannot be read

Faraday's own change, not upstream's (docs/DECISIONS.md F205). A read of EDID block 2 or later
needs three I2C messages (segment pointer, offset, read). i2c-bcm2835
starts each next message from its TXW interrupt, which holds for one
write followed by a read; with three it programs the third while the
first is on the bus, and the read ends short, on most boots of a screen
with three or more blocks. One such failure threw the whole EDID away, so
the screen had no modes. The base block and the first extension are read
with two messages.

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_edid.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_edid.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_edid.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_edid.c
@@ -2423,8 +2423,16 @@
 		edid_block_status_print(status, block, i);
 
 		if (!edid_block_status_valid(status, edid_block_tag(block))) {
-			if (status == EDID_BLOCK_READ_FAIL)
-				goto fail;
+			if (status == EDID_BLOCK_READ_FAIL) {
+				/*
+				 * The blocks read so far still describe the
+				 * screen: mark this one and those after it
+				 * invalid, and keep the others.
+				 */
+				memset(block, 0, (num_blocks - i) * EDID_LENGTH);
+				invalid_blocks += num_blocks - i;
+				break;
+			}
 			invalid_blocks++;
 		} else if (i == 1) {
 			/*
